          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How is blood pressure actually measured, and why is more than one reading needed?",
          "answer": "Blood pressure is written as two numbers, such as 120 over 80, measured in millimeters of mercury. The top number (systolic) is the pressure when the heart contracts. The bottom number (diastolic) is the pressure between beats. (Source: *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*, general clinical reference.)\n\nPressure changes all day, with activity, stress, pain, caffeine, sleep and time of day. It is usually lowest during sleep and rises before waking. Because of this, one reading is not enough. A single high reading in a clinic is not itself a diagnosis. (Source: *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*.)\n\nGuidance says a diagnosis is based on the average of two or more readings taken on two or more separate occasions. Home readings or 24-hour ambulatory monitoring (a device that records readings over a day) help confirm the diagnosis. They can also reveal two patterns that office readings alone can miss:\n- **White-coat hypertension:** readings are high in the office but normal outside it.\n- **Masked hypertension:** readings are normal in the office but high outside it, so risk can be underestimated. (Source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*, consensus guidance.)\n\nThe retrieved passages do not explain the physical measurement method, such as cuff size, arm position, or how long to rest first. A clinician or a patient-education resource can cover that. Also, the cutoff for \"high\" differs by guideline. US guidance uses 130/80 mm Hg, while European guidance starts hypertension at 140/90 mm Hg. (Source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*.)\n\nIf you have readings you are concerned about, discuss them with a clinician who can interpret them in the context of your full health picture.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
